-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### A1.3 Best Practices
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Snapshot-based validation gives stakeholders trust that the data, schema, and business meaning are preserved from legacy Hive to modern Unity Catalog tables. It’s the backbone for objective, auditable migration QA. Keep the following practices in mind when gathering snapshots:
-- MAGIC - Capture during low-activity windows to minimize changing data.
-- MAGIC - QA/sandbox environments mimic production schemas, supporting safe, representative tests.
-- MAGIC - Use versioned, immutable snapshot storage for traceability.
-- MAGIC - Automate snapshot generation and comparison for large table sets or repeated validations.
-- MAGIC - Document findings and their remediation to ensure migration quality gates are consistently met.
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A2. Schema and Data Checks
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC A systematic validation plan should verify that migrated UC tables faithfully match their Hive counterparts in structure and content. This section outlines practical checks and provides runnable examples you can adapt for your workshop.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Goals include:
-- MAGIC - Validate structural parity: columns, types, nullability, partitioning, properties, and constraints.
-- MAGIC - Validate data parity: counts, key integrity, column-level statistics, and row-level differences.
-- MAGIC - Produce actionable diffs that engineers can triage quickly.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### A2.1 What to Verify
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Schema parity
-- MAGIC    - Column names, data types, nullability
-- MAGIC    - Partitioning columns and order
-- MAGIC    - Column and table comments (if relied upon)
-- MAGIC 1. Table properties & storage
-- MAGIC    - Format (Delta expected in UC), location, owner, table type (managed/external)
-- MAGIC    - Critical TBLPROPERTIES (e.g., expectations, retention)
-- MAGIC 1. Constraints & keys
-- MAGIC    - Primary/unique keys (logical), not-null, check constraints where used
-- MAGIC 1. Data parity
-- MAGIC    - Row counts; distinct counts of keys
-- MAGIC    - Column-level: null counts, distinct counts, min/max, sums for numeric
-- MAGIC    - Row-level: missing/excess keys, mismatched values on non-key columns
-- MAGIC 1. Ignore “volatile” columns
-- MAGIC    - Columns known to change during migration (e.g., ingestion timestamps, update_ts)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="display:flex; justify-content:center;">
